# bench/bench.config -- single source of truth for the tron-goblin-node vs
# java-tron benchmark suite.
#
# This file is SOURCED (not executed) by every runner (bootstrap.sh, run.sh,
# ours.sh, java.sh, decode/*.sh, rpc/*.sh). It declares every external input the
# suite needs, with portable defaults, and lets each one be overridden from the
# environment:
#
#     SNAPSHOT_PATH=/big-disk/my-snapshot BENCH_WORK=/big-disk/bench-work \
#         bench/run.sh
#
# Every assignment below uses `${VAR:-default}`, so an environment value (or a
# value exported by a runner before sourcing) always wins over the default.
#
# ISOLATION GUARANTEE: the suite only ever READS the snapshot at SNAPSHOT_PATH
# (read-only) and COPIES it into a working data-dir under BENCH_WORK. It never
# hard-links, resets, moves, or writes any path outside BENCH_WORK. There are no
# hardcoded machine paths, no shared "pristine" directory, and no LAN IPs here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Repo / suite locations (derived; not normally overridden).
#
# BENCH_DIR and REPO_ROOT are computed by each runner before sourcing this file
# (they `export BENCH_DIR=...` first), so the defaults below only apply when the
# file is sourced standalone (e.g. for inspection).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BENCH_DIR="${BENCH_DIR:-$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}")" && pwd)}"
REPO_ROOT="${REPO_ROOT:-$(cd "${BENCH_DIR}/.." && pwd)}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BENCH_WORK -- the ISOLATED working directory the suite fully owns.
#
# Everything the suite creates lives here: the built java-tron jar, the JDK
# hint, per-engine writable data-dirs (copied from the snapshot), the fetched
# decode corpus, logs, and samples. Point it at a big-disk path when the
# default (bench/.work, gitignored) is on a small filesystem -- a copied
# snapshot is as large as the source snapshot (often 70+ GiB), and the suite
# keeps a separate copy per engine per dimension.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BENCH_WORK="${BENCH_WORK:-${BENCH_DIR}/.work}"

# Results directory (per-engine JSON + generated REPORT.md). Small; kept under
# the suite by default so reports are easy to find.
RESULTS_DIR="${RESULTS_DIR:-${BENCH_DIR}/results}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Our node (tron-goblin-node).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release binary, built by `cargo build --release -p tron-node` (bootstrap.sh
# does this). Override TRON_NODE to point at a prebuilt binary.
TRON_NODE="${TRON_NODE:-${REPO_ROOT}/target/release/tron-node}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# java-tron (the reference engine).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The vanilla java-tron release to clone + build. bootstrap.sh clones the
# upstream repo at this git tag and runs `:framework:buildFullNodeJar`. This is
# stock, un-instrumented java-tron -- no diagnostic edits.
JAVA_TRON_TAG="${JAVA_TRON_TAG:-GreatVoyage-v4.8.1.1}"

# Upstream java-tron git remote bootstrap.sh clones from.
JAVA_TRON_REPO_URL="${JAVA_TRON_REPO_URL:-https://github.com/tronprotocol/java-tron.git}"

# Path to a PREBUILT vanilla FullNode jar. If set and present, bootstrap.sh
# skips the clone + gradle build entirely and the java runners use this jar.
# Leave empty to have bootstrap.sh build one into BENCH_WORK.
JAVA_TRON_JAR="${JAVA_TRON_JAR:-}"

# Where bootstrap.sh clones java-tron and where it puts the built jar (both
# under BENCH_WORK so the suite owns them). The java runners read JAVA_TRON_JAR
# if set, else this built jar.
JT_SRC_DIR="${JT_SRC_DIR:-${BENCH_WORK}/java-tron-src}"
JT_BUILT_JAR="${JT_BUILT_JAR:-${BENCH_WORK}/java/FullNode.jar}"

# JDK 8 home -- java-tron GreatVoyage builds and runs under JDK 8. By default
# the suite uses whatever `javac`/`java` are on PATH (set JAVA_HOME to a JDK 8
# before running, or override JDK8_HOME to point at a specific JDK 8 install).
# bootstrap.sh verifies the resolved java reports a 1.8 version.
JDK8_HOME="${JDK8_HOME:-${JAVA_HOME:-}}"

# JVM heap for the java engine. java-tron normally runs a large pre-touched
# heap; -Xms=-Xmx with AlwaysPreTouch inflates RSS vs a native process, which
# the report and READMEs call out honestly. Lower it to compare under a
# smaller-heap java configuration.
XMX="${XMX:-24g}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Snapshot -- the shared pre-state both engines start from.
#
# SNAPSHOT_PATH must point at a TRON LiteFullNode snapshot directory you supply
# (a java-format RocksDB tree whose stores live under <snapshot>/database/).
# The suite reads it READ-ONLY and copies it into per-engine data-dirs under
# BENCH_WORK. It is NEVER modified.
#
# If SNAPSHOT_PATH is unset/missing and SNAPSHOT_URL is set, bootstrap.sh
# downloads + extracts the archive at SNAPSHOT_URL into BENCH_WORK/snapshot and
# uses that. Otherwise bootstrap.sh fails loudly with instructions.
#
# Public mainnet LiteFullNode snapshots are published by the TRON community,
# e.g. http://34.86.86.229/ (snapshot index). Pick a LiteFullNode (not a
# FullNode/archive) snapshot; it is smaller and matches this suite's read
# paths.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SNAPSHOT_PATH="${SNAPSHOT_PATH:-${BENCH_WORK}/snapshot}"
SNAPSHOT_URL="${SNAPSHOT_URL:-}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sync peer / seed (the sync dimension).
#
# SYNC_PEER is how both engines obtain blocks for the sync race:
#   * EMPTY (default): use built-in peer DISCOVERY. Our node walks the TRON
#     mainnet DNS tree (main.trondisco.net) + Kademlia DHT to find public
#     peers; java-tron uses its own discovery + the public seed nodes below.
#     This is fully portable -- no node you control is required.
#   * "host:port" : pin a single upstream peer (discovery off) for both
#     engines. Use this when you have a fast local/known mainnet peer; it makes
#     the network path identical and reduces sync-time variance. It MUST be a
#     reachable mainnet p2p peer (port 18888) -- never a private LAN IP baked
#     into the suite.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SYNC_PEER="${SYNC_PEER:-}"

# Public TRON mainnet seed nodes for the java engine's discovery when SYNC_PEER
# is empty (java-tron needs at least a seed list to bootstrap discovery). These
# are the stock seed nodes from java-tron's own main_net_config.conf. Override
# JAVA_SEED_NODES (newline- or space-separated host:port list) to change them.
JAVA_SEED_NODES="${JAVA_SEED_NODES:-3.225.171.164:18888 52.53.189.99:18888 18.196.99.16:18888 34.253.187.192:18888 18.133.82.227:18888 35.180.51.163:18888 54.252.224.209:18888 18.231.27.82:18888 52.15.93.92:18888 34.220.77.106:18888 13.127.47.162:18888 13.124.62.58:18888 13.229.128.108:18888 35.182.37.246:18888 34.200.228.125:18888 18.220.232.201:18888 13.211.142.31:18888 13.231.4.243:18888 39.107.80.135:18888 100.20.71.74:18888}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Block range (sync dimension) + decode corpus size.
#
# The defaults span the divergence-hunt window this suite was originally tuned
# for; ANY range works as long as the supplied snapshot's head sits at
# (FROM - 1). The runners warn if the imported head does not match FROM.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM="${FROM:-83316753}"
TO="${TO:-83386262}"

# Decode-corpus block count (decode dimension): the first DECODE_COUNT blocks of
# the corpus are decoded in each pass.
DECODE_COUNT="${DECODE_COUNT:-10000}"

# Decode corpus file (length-prefixed [int32-be length][Block bytes], ascending
# order). bootstrap.sh fetches the [FROM, TO] blocks into this path via our
# node's fetch-block path. Override BLOCKS_FILE to supply your own corpus.
BLOCKS_FILE="${BLOCKS_FILE:-${BENCH_WORK}/corpus/blocks_${FROM}-${TO}.blocks}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RPC dimension knobs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTP /wallet/* host:port both engines serve and the load generator hits.
HTTP_HOST="${HTTP_HOST:-127.0.0.1}"
HTTP_PORT="${HTTP_PORT:-8090}"

# Steady-state idle-RSS sample window (seconds, no load) and the health-wait
# timeouts (java cold-starts slower than ours).
IDLE_SAMPLE_S="${IDLE_SAMPLE_S:-5}"
HEALTH_TIMEOUT_OURS_S="${HEALTH_TIMEOUT_OURS_S:-60}"
HEALTH_TIMEOUT_JAVA_S="${HEALTH_TIMEOUT_JAVA_S:-180}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Derived per-engine, per-dimension working data-dirs (all under BENCH_WORK).
# Each is a SEPARATE copy of the snapshot the engine may write freely; the
# source snapshot is never touched. Override individually if needed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SYNC_OURS_DATA="${SYNC_OURS_DATA:-${BENCH_WORK}/data/sync-ours}"
SYNC_JAVA_DATA="${SYNC_JAVA_DATA:-${BENCH_WORK}/data/sync-java}"
RPC_OURS_DATA="${RPC_OURS_DATA:-${BENCH_WORK}/data/rpc-ours}"
RPC_JAVA_DATA="${RPC_JAVA_DATA:-${BENCH_WORK}/data/rpc-java}"
